  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nA significant pattern shift is still anticipated this weekend \nthrough early next week as the most of the deterministic and \nensemble model guidance continues to show a broad troughing pattern \nsetting up across much of the western third of the CONUS. Guidance \ncontinues to show a northern stream trough diving into the Pacific \nNorthwest on Saturday before sinking southward over CA on Sunday. \nMeanwhile, a more southern stream shortwave trough is likely to move \ninto the subtropical eastern Pacific and pick up the remnants of \nHurricane Rachel as it moves northeastward and phases with the west \ncoast trough. As this whole pattern evolution takes shape, the \nincreasing south to southwesterly flow aloft will advect moisture \ninto the Desert Southwest starting Saturday, with most of the \nmoisture at first confined in the mid to upper-levels before better \nlow-level moisture moves in on Sunday as the remnants of Rachel gets \npulled in. \n\nCoincidentally, rain chances will be on the increase as early as \nSaturday afternoon across south-central AZ before peaking Sunday \ninto Monday across much of the region with the arrival of better low-\nlevel moisture. There continues to be a great deal of uncertainty in \nrainfall amounts as it will be dependent on the overall track of the \nremnants of Rachel and how it interacts with the west coast trough. \nIt will likely take at least another couple of days to get a better \nidea on the rainfall perspectives as guidance comes into better \nagreement on how the overall pattern shapes up. In addition to the \nrainfall potential, winds will be another hazard to contend with as \nthe combination of the height falls from the trough and remnant \ncirculation of Rachel will likely result in widespread breeziness.\n\nAs 500 mb heights lower with the approaching trough along with \nthe increasing cloud cover and moisture, much cooler temperatures\nare expected this weekend into early next week. Afternoon highs \nacross the lower deserts will go from mid to upper 90s on Saturday\nto low to mid 80s by Monday and may potentially drop even lower \non Tuesday with many locations stuck in the low 80s.",
